#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7da551-fc32-5399-967a-ca52f4de957a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292091} {:answer/id #uuid "d11c9e26-adff-5a42-bbcb-d4dc4a59cb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292092} {:answer/id #uuid "91b18fc2-d4f8-576c-95ba-ab19794784eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292093} {:answer/id #uuid "4a538e83-fa76-5561-b11d-5d460fa79700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-4t,\\,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292094} {:answer/id #uuid "00fe46db-a3d4-5b1b-a9b8-b17856c473cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe46db-a3d4-5b1b-a9b8-b17856c473cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292095} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b11901-df19-53f7-b179-442583776fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "914c8844-0878-5387-a79a-b5ca32d2ccb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106462" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ -4 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8823679c-4206-593e-9924-bba1b808b386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288397} {:answer/id #uuid "9445efe6-ebcf-5fed-8a56-5ce164ddda33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288398} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce72c8-fabe-53b4-9353-dea6391c44b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399} {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} {:answer/id #uuid "32999de3-d390-5e7b-8e44-6029adc7ac8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f933ccb-75fa-51e5-b46c-a9bcbb9b8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bd091d-91d9-5843-968d-693b832cddd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efd48b95-db5f-52c0-99e2-d4c8610ebe36" :question/math-academy-id "q-106465" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29acce41-c002-5d4b-8e0c-899e03b85800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29acce41-c002-5d4b-8e0c-899e03b85800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335666} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ae4835-b693-58ed-ba98-152de30a9ad6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a2a18b-c37c-5810-9b54-40770c679e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a2a18b-c37c-5810-9b54-40770c679e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335668} :answer-field/id #uuid "444f7d45-5653-53bd-904d-da9149a5e623" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b38783e-1aa6-5722-8348-08e33fa245db" :question/math-academy-id "q-321592" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6678f3a-fe84-51f2-9aeb-44be1c9af076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5 = 3t^{2}, y + 1 = 6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155957} {:answer/id #uuid "0d796cb1-ffa4-5851-9034-b80275566fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 1 = 3t, y - 5 = 6t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155958} {:answer/id #uuid "32c1972c-dbf6-5f3b-aec9-e0240ba92f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5 = 6t, y + 1 = 3t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155959} {:answer/id #uuid "3856b5a2-7993-5136-abeb-33c5a135d134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 =-3t^{2}, y + 5 =-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155960} {:answer/id #uuid "55fc3c8b-d686-54de-94ec-91a1313f1378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 5 = 6t^{2}, y - 1 = 3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6678f3a-fe84-51f2-9aeb-44be1c9af076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 5 = 3t^{2}, y + 1 = 6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155957} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32f70b8-7dd3-534f-9453-b848ca3346bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "367eead1-51e7-5f05-9cdc-3a8e5cc1c6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-84467" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e78fc381-1d46-548b-81d4-3c22b2a741b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335671} {:answer/id #uuid "decc5de0-73b9-5ba6-813f-b8ee87a392e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335672} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbffa8e-abed-5a51-a13a-d1d32514a346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-6(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335673} {:answer/id #uuid "01ca4d8d-f5bd-5d8c-bcd6-1a21ad35a1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335674} {:answer/id #uuid "05d47e87-8d94-5722-bcc2-821195510438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "decc5de0-73b9-5ba6-813f-b8ee87a392e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335672} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9acdab2-5744-579e-baad-36edcbaf391e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47d76f1d-d242-5bf6-8664-6b58d2510ca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84855" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-3=-6t,\\,y-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-3}{6},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-3}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{y-1}{3} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{y-1}{3}) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
